# Engineering contract

Repository: `{{repository}}`

Verified origin: `{{origin}}`

Factory source: [{{revision}}]({{factory_url}}/tree/{{revision}})

The shared operating rules in `AGENTS.md` are available locally to any selected AI engineering client; verify its instruction-loading path. Pinned factory references supplement them. Complete this app-owned contract with actual decisions, commands and evidence as the project develops.

## Product and architecture

Record the product outcome, intended users and acceptance criteria. Choose only required components, runtime, hosting and integrations. Document relevant interfaces, data ownership, configuration boundaries, authentication/authorization and compatibility. If the app needs persistence or jobs, include durability/migration/restore or execution/retry/output behavior as applicable.

## Delivery state and decisions

Keep a compact current record of the goal and agreed acceptance, design decisions and user answers, material inferred assumptions and their basis, and postponed decisions with the reason and concrete reopening trigger. Update it as codesign, prototype feedback and implementation change the solution. This is agent-maintained continuity, not a form the user must fill in.

Record the latest durable branch/commit, completed and remaining work, actual local/CI/hosted verification boundaries, deployment state, blockers and specific next actions. Keep existing authorization and private operation/resource references available through approved private records; do not copy credentials or private provider IDs here. Reconcile this state at the next session before reopening decisions or retrying operations. Check actual source/runtime revisions; an older snapshot or instruction seed may need a deliberate update.

For each requested milestone, record its actual state (not started, attempted, confirmed, blocked, stopped or unknown outcome) and its evidence. Distinguish local initialization, provider-verified existing remote repository, implemented/committed code, remote push/PR, completed checks and a verified live user flow. For blockers, separate safe observed errors from hypotheses, record the diagnostic budget and what must change before retrying. Preserve explicit stops across continuation and separate new tasks; a proposed next action is not permission to resume stopped work.

## Commands and verification

Document the actual setup, development, execution, validation or packaging commands appropriate to the workload. Maintain relevant dependency/tool pins, lockfiles and app-owned CI/CD. Verify meaningful behavior, failures and required integration in the intended runtime; distinguish local checks, CI and hosted acceptance. Browser, API, typecheck and build commands apply only when relevant. Keep secrets out of source/client bundles/logs and use reviewed app-owned CI authentication.

## Hosting, operation and continuation

Record the agreed targets, deployment/configuration process, source/build identities, exposure/protection and relevant operational checks. Actual secret values and provider IDs stay in approved private records; list non-secret configuration names and private references here. Include rollback/retry, monitoring, cleanup ownership and any data recovery requirements.

When custom DNS is required, retain the verified public-zone ID/name and exact managed records in approved private app records. Document CREATE-first planning, creation versus external reuse, core exclusions, provider change IDs/readback, HTTPS and browser acceptance, and owned-record cleanup. Route53 inventory is operational ownership evidence, not record-level IAM tag isolation.

Document how a fresh task clones this repository, restores approved private configuration, runs relevant commands and reconciles existing resources. Keep source durable through approved Git push or private export with restoration proof. Record project/source/run provenance and actual acceptance results; tool installation or identity reads do not prove hosted behavior.

